Local fixes: three small patches. 0001-host-build-compat.patch is two
host-build-compatibility fixes (mmc.c's compiler allowlist didn't
include a host-build option; mmc.h used size_t without including
<stddef.h>, relying on <xc.h> to pull it in transitively, which a host
build skips). 0002-crc-stdio-guard.patch scopes crc.c's <stdio.h>
include to the PC_CODE_TO_GENERATE_THE_TABLES table-generator guard
(always false): under epic-cc the unconditional include pulls the
driver's stdio formatter, whose va_start the PIC18 backend cannot
lower. All are cosmetic/portability, not functional changes. See
epic-sdcard/third_party/patches/ (the exact diffs, kept reapplicable)
and epic-sdcard/README.md, for details.

To upgrade to a newer upstream commit:
  1. git clone the URL above at the new commit.
  2. Replace storage/include/{crc.h,mmc.h} and storage/src/{crc.c,mmc.c}
     in this directory with the fresh clone's copies (keep this
     VENDORED_COMMIT file and epic-sdcard/third_party/patches/, update the
     commit hash/date above).
  3. cd to the repo root and run:
       patch -p0 --dry-run < epic-sdcard/third_party/patches/0001-host-build-compat.patch
       patch -p0 --dry-run < epic-sdcard/third_party/patches/0002-crc-stdio-guard.patch
     (note: -p0, not -p1 -- the patches' paths already include the
     "epic-sdcard/" prefix). If a patch applies cleanly, apply for real
     (drop --dry-run). If a hunk fails, check whether upstream already
     fixed that issue before re-deriving it.
  4. Re-run the host build (cmake -B build && cmake --build build &&
     ctest --test-dir build) and the real xc8-cc compile (see
     epic-sdcard/README.md's Quick start for the build commands)
     before trusting the upgrade.
